g precision when writing the WKT string. Set to a value of\n    57→        -1 to indicate the full precision.\n    58→    trim : bool, default True\n    59→        If True, trim unnecessary decimals (trailing zeros).\n    60→    output_dimension : int, default None\n    61→        The output dimension for the WKT string. Supported values are 2, 3 and\n    62→        4 for GEOS 3.12+. Default None will automatically choose 3 or 4,\n    63→        depending on the version of GEOS.\n    64→        Specifying 3 means that up to 3 dimensions will be written but 2D\n    65→        geometries will still be represented as 2D in the WKT string.\n    66→    old_3d : bool, default False\n    67→        Enable old style 3D/4D WKT generation. By default, new style 3D/4D WKT\n    68→        (ie. \"POINT Z (10 20 30)\") is returned, but with ``old_3d=True``\n    69→        the WKT will be formatted in the style \"POINT (10 20 30)\".\n    70→    **kwargs\n    71→        See :ref:`NumPy ufunc docs <ufuncs.kwargs>` for other keyword arguments.\n    72→\n    73→    Examples\n    74→    --------\n    75→    >>> from shapely import Point\n    76→    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0))\n    77→    'POINT (0 0)'\n    78→    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0), rounding_precision=3, trim=False)\n    79→    'POINT (0.000 0.000)'\n    80→    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0), rounding_precision=-1, trim=False)\n    81→    'POINT (0.0000000000000000 0.0000000000000000)'\n    82→    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True)\n    83→    'POINT Z (1 2 3)'\n    84→    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True, output_dimension=2)\n    85→    'POINT (1 2)'\n    86→    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True, old_3d=True)\n    87→    'POINT (1 2 3)'\n    88→\n    89→    Notes\n    90→    -----\n    91→    The defaults differ from the default of the GEOS library. To mimic this,\n    92→    use::\n    93→\n    94→        to_wkt(geometry, rounding_precision=-1, trim=False, output_dimension=2)\n    95→\n    96→    \"\"\"\n    97→    if not np.isscal