rgument)\n  1200\u2192\n  1201\u2192    Examples\n  1202\u2192    --------\n  1203\u2192    >>> from shapely import Point\n  1204\u2192    >>> point = Point(0.5, 0.5)\n  1205\u2192    >>> dwithin(point, Point(2, 0.5), 2)\n  1206\u2192    True\n  1207\u2192    >>> dwithin(point, Point(2, 0.5), [2, 1.5, 1]).tolist()\n  1208\u2192    [True, True, False]\n  1209\u2192    >>> dwithin(point, Point(0.5, 0.5), 0)\n  1210\u2192    True\n  1211\u2192    >>> dwithin(point, None, 100)\n  1212\u2192    False\n  1213\u2192\n  1214\u2192    \"\"\"\n  1215\u2192    return lib.dwithin(a, b, distance, **kwargs)\n  1216\u2192\n  1217\u2192\n  1218\u2192@multithreading_enabled\n  1219\u2192def contains_xy(geom, x, y=None, **kwargs):\n  1220\u2192    \"\"\"Return True if the Point (x, y) is completely inside geom.\n  1221\u2192\n  1222\u2192    This is a special-case (and faster) variant of the `contains` function\n  1223\u2192    which avoids having to create a Point object if you start from x/y\n  1224\u2192    coordinates.\n  1225\u2192\n  1226\u2192    Note that in the case of points, the `contains_properly` predicate is\n  1227\u2192    equivalent to `contains`.\n  1228\u2192\n  1229\u2192    See the docstring of `contains` for more details about the predicate.\n  1230\u2192\n  1231\u2192    Parameters\n  1232\u2192    ----------\n  1233\u2192    geom : Geometry or array_like\n  1234\u2192        Geometry or geometries to check if they contain the point.\n  1235\u2192    x, y : float or array_like\n  1236\u2192        Coordinates as separate x and y arrays, or a single array of\n  1237\u2192        coordinate x, y tuples.\n  1238\u2192    **kwargs\n  1239\u2192        See :ref:`NumPy ufunc docs <ufuncs.kwargs>` for other keyword arguments.\n  1240\u2192\n  1241\u2192    See Also\n  1242\u2192    --------\n  1243\u2192    contains : variant taking two geometries as input\n  1244\u2192\n  1245\u2192    Notes\n  1246\u2192    -----\n  1247\u2192    If you compare a small number of pol